From: Local build <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] EXP: drm: test an asynchronous cursor move once, not at every pointer event (KWIN_CURSOR_TEST_EVERY_MOVE)

An asynchronous cursor update (the cursor layer's repaintScheduled handler in compositor.cpp,
then DrmPipeline::presentAsync) builds and tests the whole atomic state of the pipeline (a
TEST_ONLY commit) before it queues the cursor plane's new state. Item::setPosition() schedules
the cursor layer's repaint before and after the move, for each item of the cursor (the cursor
image, and at fractional scales often the zero-size CursorItem itself, through rounding), so
every pointer event makes two to four such updates, and the first one carries the old position.
With an 8000 Hz virtual pointer, kwin_wayland's main thread made 25,000-30,000 TEST_ONLY atomic
commits a second on a Radeon 780M, 8-32 us each in the kernel: about 0.4-0.5 s per second of
pointer motion (2026-10-17).

presentAsync now drops an update whose plane state equals the one it queued last, and reuses its
last successful test when only the plane's position changed: same framebuffer, source rectangle,
size, hotspot, transform and zpos, the same crtc edges crossed, and on each crossed axis at least
64 px visible before and after (drivers restrict plane positions only at the edges: i915 refuses
the cursor across the left edge of CHV's pipe C, amdgpu a plane with less than 24x12 px visible).
Every other test or commit of the pipeline (each composited frame tests and commits), applying or
reverting pending changes, and a failed commit in the commit thread reset it. The cursor is then
tested at most once per composited frame, per new cursor image and per edge class entered,
whatever the input rate. The commit thread is unchanged: it does not test a commit before
submitting it, and it tests the commits it reorders.

KWIN_CURSOR_TEST_EVERY_MOVE=1 restores the previous behaviour; =2 drops unchanged updates but
tests every other one. A host KWin keeps its wrapper's environment from login, so
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-cursor-test-every-move holding 0, 1 or 2 overrides the variable while it
exists (read at most once a second: A/B in a running session). An upstream version would drop
both. See findings/kwin-cursor-atomic-tests-2026-10-17.md.

